{"reference":"Matthew 19","book":"Matthew","chapter":19,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Literary and Interpretive Question","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The opening formula, “when Jesus had finished these words,” is one of five repeated formulas that organize Matthew’s larger collections of teaching. It marks a transition here, though readers should not assume that the five-fold pattern proves a single, universally accepted symbolic outline. The discussion of divorce, including its exception language, and the threefold saying about eunuchs have also been understood in differing ways; the passage itself places them within teaching about marriage, children, and discipleship.","verse_refs":["Matthew 19:1","Matthew 19:1-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_019_bbf48ad7e7d612fea9187dae"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}